WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
Change one base in a gene, and the protein may not change at all.

2
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.000
Add one base, and every amino acid after it can change.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.120
Both facts follow from one rule.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.980
The cell reads a message of four bases, called messenger RNA.

5
00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:27.923
Pairs of bases give only 16 words, too few for 20 amino acids.

6
00:00:28.823 --> 00:00:31.463
Triplets give 64, more than enough.

7
00:00:32.323 --> 00:00:37.943
A codon is a group of three bases that specifies one amino acid or a stop.

8
00:00:38.763 --> 00:00:43.963
Of the 64, 61 stand for amino acids and 3 mean stop.

9
00:00:44.903 --> 00:00:49.823
Reading usually starts at the first AUG, and runs on in groups of three.

10
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:54.597
Each codon is looked up in the genetic code.

11
00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:02.997
AUG is methionine, GCU is alanine, and UGG is tryptophan.

12
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:11.497
AAA is lysine, CCU is proline, and GAC is aspartate.

13
00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.957
Then UAG is a stop, and it adds no amino acid.

14
00:01:17.937 --> 00:01:20.137
The chain is six residues long.

15
00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:23.217
Now change one base.

16
00:01:24.257 --> 00:01:30.857
First, GCU to GCC still means alanine, so the change is silent.

17
00:01:32.037 --> 00:01:39.817
Second, GCU to ACU means threonine, so one amino acid swaps, a missense change.

18
00:01:41.197 --> 00:01:49.737
Third, UGG to UGA is a stop, so the chain ends after two residues, a nonsense change.

19
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:54.837
Now add one base, a G, right after the start codon.

20
00:01:55.497 --> 00:01:56.977
The start codon stays.

21
00:01:57.997 --> 00:02:02.057
Every base after the new G slides along by one place.

22
00:02:02.877 --> 00:02:09.197
The triplets regroup, and the protein becomes methionine, glycine, leucine,

23
00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:11.437
glutamate, threonine.

24
00:02:12.477 --> 00:02:17.897
Then a stop that was out of frame now comes early, and the protein is shorter.

25
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.160
Crick and colleagues studied this in phage T4, and called the two kinds of mutation

26
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.920
plus and minus. Picture its gene as a sentence of three-letter words.

27
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.320
A plus with a nearby minus often restored function.

28
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:42.120
Two pluses did not, but three pluses restored wild or nearly wild growth in the

29
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.260
combinations they tested.

30
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:50.820
Keep this rule: one base more or less moves the frame, and the frame sets every codon.
